  I'll put in the spec's Global Constraints, along with a linter/formatter and a unit-test runner from the start —
  cheapest to add now, before there's any code.

  Now the question that actually defines your feature, because "tasks they care about" is doing all the work in your
  original sentence. Three ways to make it concrete:

  - [1mImplicit[0m — you care about tasks you created or are assigned to. No new table, no UI; the notification fan-out is a
    query over the task's own fields. Cheapest. The cost is that caring is not something a user can decline: you cannot
    stop getting notified about a task you're assigned to, short of unassigning yourself.
  - [1mExplicit watch list[0m — a [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m table with a watch/unwatch toggle, auto-populated when you create or get
    assigned a task. This is the GitHub model. Slightly more code, but "who gets notified" becomes an inspectable row in
    a table rather than logic buried in the fan-out, and mute works.
  - [1mPer-user preferences on top[0m — explicit watching plus per-event-type filters ("only notify me on status changes, not
    comments"). Most flexible; adds a preferences model and a settings UI before you've learned what people actually
    want muted.

  I recommend the explicit watch list. It's maybe thirty extra lines over the implicit version, and it buys the one
  thing the implicit model structurally cannot do — let someone stop caring. It also keeps the notifications subsystem
  honest: it reads subscriptions, it doesn't re-derive who cares from task internals, so the two pieces stay separable.
  I'd skip per-event preferences until something is annoying; that's a YAGNI cut I'd rather make now and revisit with
  evidence.
